  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe persistence of deep layer subsidence will hold across Lower Mi \ninto Tuesday under a blocked long wave pattern across eastern North \nAmerica. While some retrogression of the upper low over the Mid \nAtlantic will drive a few high clouds toward eastern Mi from time to \ntime, ample sunshine will prevail. Forecast mixing depths on model \nsoundings and probabilistic guidance supports daytime highs generally \nranging from the upper 60s to lower 70s Sunday through at least \nTuesday. There will be slight increase in the north-northeasterly \ngradient flow across Lake Huron tonight into Sunday as the Mid \nAtlantic system tightens the gradient over the lower Great Lakes. \nThis will advect a little higher Lake Huron moisture into the \nboundary layer, raising the prospects for fog development both \ntonight and Sunday night. Recent HRRR runs are quite bullish in \nsuggesting fog development over a large portions of the northern \nforecast area. Modified Richardson numbers do suggest some weak \nboundary layer stability despite increasing low level winds, enough \nto warrant a mention of fog in the north both tonight and Sun night.\n\nThe blocking pattern is forecast to break down Tues into Wed \nallowing a northern stream trough to slide across the northern Great \nLakes. Model variability in the amplitude of this wave and the \ndegree to which it breaks down the ridging across the eastern lakes \non Wednesday raises a good deal of uncertainty as to the efficiency \nwith which it drives moisture into Se Mi. This uncertainty warrants just\nchance type pops the middle of next week. It does appear the \neventual breakdown of the ridge will allow a west to east oriented \nlow to mid level baroclinic zone to settle across the Great Lakes \nand/or Ohio Valley. There is good agreement within the model prob \nspace in phasing the remnant tropical system from the Pacific and a \nsouthern stream wave, which drive a tropical moisture plume into the \nsouthern and central plains by Wednesday. Although deterministic \nmodel solutions indicate a weakening of this southern stream wave as \nit lifts into the upper Midwest/Great Lakes toward the end of the \nforecast period, the tropical moisture plume intersecting the \naforementioned frontal boundary draped across the Great Lakes suggest\nthe potential for ample rainfall to parts of the region, warranting \na chance of rain during the end of the work week.",
